    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm studying for the USMLE and I'm a bit stuck on the RAAS pathway. Can you explain the mechanism of action for ACE inhibitors, like lisinopril? Specifically, how do they interact with the system to lower blood pressure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ACE inhibitors, like lisinopril, work by blocking the Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE). This enzyme is crucial in the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) because it converts Angiotensin I into Angiotensin II. Angiotensin II is a potent vasoconstrictor, meaning it tightens blood vessels, which raises blood pressure. By inhibiting ACE, these drugs prevent the formation of Angiotensin II, leading to vasodilation and a subsequent drop in blood pressure. Furthermore, they also reduce aldosterone secretion, which helps decrease sodium and water retention.",
      "source": "target",
