    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go into a bit more detail about the actual binding process? Like, how does the molecule of the inhibitor physically interact with the enzyme to stop the conversion from happening? I want to make sure I understand the biochemical inhibition part.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The binding process is a classic example of competitive inhibition, though it's often described as a transition-state analog mimicry. The ACE enzyme has an active site where Angiotensin I normally binds. Lisinopril, or its active metabolite, structurally resembles the substrate (Angiotensin I) and fits into this active site. It then forms strong, non-covalent bonds—like hydrogen bonds and hydrophobic interactions—with key amino acid residues within the enzyme's active pocket, effectively jamming the machinery. This physical occupancy prevents Angiotensin I from being processed into Angiotensin II.",
      "source": "target",
