    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, I think I follow the competitive inhibition part. So it's basically just occupying the space so the real substrate can't get in. Is there any difference in how the body handles that \"jammed\" enzyme compared to other types of inhibitors, or does it just stay stuck there until the enzyme is degraded or anything?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's not just a simple blockade; some of these inhibitors are designed to be potent, quasi-irreversible binders, though they are typically reversible inhibitors in the pharmacological sense. When lisinopril binds, it creates a stable complex with the enzyme, effectively rendering it inactive for the duration of the drug's action. Unlike some irreversible inhibitors that might form a permanent covalent bond, the ACE-inhibitor-enzyme complex eventually dissociates, but the drug's concentration in the system dictates how quickly the enzyme becomes functional again. The body handles this by maintaining homeostasis, but the effect is sustained as long as the drug is present.",
      "source": "target",
